<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The theme BridgeActivity switches to once the WebView is up (postSplashScreenTheme
      below). windowBackground is load-bearing twice over, which is why it is the frame
      colour and no longer @null:
        1. Capacitor's core SystemBars plugin does
           decorView.setBackgroundColor(getThemeColor(context, android.R.attr.windowBackground))
           in setStyle — so this is literally the colour painted behind the transparent
           status and navigation bars.
        2. It is what shows in the gap between the splash tearing down and the WebView's
           first paint. With @null that gap was Android white against a dark leather app —
           the white flash MC4 exists to remove.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/scriptorium_frame</item>
    </style>


    <!--
      Launch theme, applied by AndroidManifest.xml to MainActivity (androidx
      core-splashscreen, see coreSplashScreenVersion in android/variables.gradle).
      minSdkVersion is 24, so two eras have to be covered — and both windowSplashScreen*
      attrs below reach both of them, by two entirely different routes:

      - API 31+ (the device under test is API 36): Theme.SplashScreen's values-v31 variant
        forwards ?windowSplashScreenBackground / ?windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon into the
        platform's android:windowSplashScreen* attrs, and the system reads them off the
        launch theme before the activity exists. Note this works without anyone calling
        installSplashScreen() — which Capacitor's BridgeActivity does not do.
      - API 24..30: the library's Theme.SplashScreen.Common sets android:windowBackground to
        @drawable/compat_splash_screen_no_icon_background. That resource has two variants in
        the AAR, and with minSdkVersion 24 the one that always resolves is the
        res/drawable-v23/ one; the bare colour drawable in res/drawable/ is only selected
        below API 23, so it is dead code here. The v23 variant is not a colour but a
        layer-list of three items: ?attr/windowSplashScreenBackground filling the window,
        then ?windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon centred at
        @dimen/splashscreen_icon_size_no_background, then an oval stroke painted in the
        same background colour that crops the mark down to a circle.
        So the same frame colour lands there too, and the mark is drawn on 24..30 as well:
        circle masked by that compat stroke rather than by the platform.

      windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon is therefore live on EVERY supported API level, not
      only 31+. Changing or dropping it changes the pre-31 splash too, silently.
      It takes the *adaptive foreground* (transparent outside the mark), not
      @mipmap/ic_launcher: the frame colour is painted beneath it either way (by the
      platform on 31+, by the layer-list's first item below that) and the icon ends up
      circle masked in both eras, so the legacy icon's baked-in frame square would only
      add a seam.

      Two items are kept despite being inert, both inherited from Capacitor's template:
      - android:background is a View attribute, not a window one, so @drawable/splash is not
        what paints the pre-31 launch screen. That is the android:windowBackground this
        style inherits from Theme.SplashScreen.Common, described just above. It is NOT the
        android:windowBackground declared in AppTheme.NoActionBar earlier in this file:
        the name here reads as if it implicitly extended that style (Android's dot notation
        would make AppTheme.NoActionBar its parent), but the explicit parent= attribute
        below overrides the name, so this style descends from Theme.SplashScreen only and
        inherits nothing at all from AppTheme.NoActionBar. The item stays because it is
        Capacitor's generated line and because @drawable/splash must keep resolving;
        drawable/splash.xml draws the same frame + mark should it ever be used.
      - postSplashScreenTheme is only read inside installSplashScreen(), which BridgeActivity
        never calls — it does setTheme(AppTheme_NoActionBar) itself in onCreate, so the
        handoff happens regardless. Declared so the intent is explicit and so the theme keeps
        working if the activity ever adopts the library's normal entry point.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/splash</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/scriptorium_frame</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>
